    "growth_rate_source": "judgment: mid-cycle production growth (Lower 48 + Willow first oil 2029 + LNG ramp) at a flat real price deck",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (10.3% 3y / 16.7% 5y) diverges >30% from revenue (-9.1% / +25.7%) and EPS (-24.2% / n.m.). None of these is a growth rate: the 3y window runs from the FY2022 price peak, the 5y window from the FY2020 COVID trough (negative EPS and owner FCF, so 5y EPS/FCF CAGRs are undefined), and equity growth is inflated by two all-stock acquisitions (Concho 2021, Marathon Oil 2024).",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award taxes): FY20 ~-102, FY22 ~17,728, FY25 6,807. SBC is small (0.4-0.6% of revenue) and the owner/reported gap is ~6%, so the SBC adjustment does not change the growth story; the price cycle does."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Oil settles back into a $65-70 WTI / ~$4 Henry Hub mid-cycle after the 2026 rally. COP delivers the incremental $1bn cost target and holds the Marathon synergies, so margins edge back to the ~45% EBITDA it earned on comparable decks in FY19 and FY23. Willow reaches first oil in early 2029 near its revised $8.5-9.0bn budget and Port Arthur/Qatar LNG ramp on a months-not-years delay, adding 3-4%/yr of revenue through FY2030 before Lower 48 maturity slows growth to ~2%. Capex falls from the Willow peak to sustaining levels roughly equal to DD&A.",
    "bull": "A structurally tighter market - LNG demand outrunning the Qatar/US Gulf supply wave and OPEC+ spare capacity absorbed - keeps realised prices near $75-80 WTI. Lower cost structure plus price torque lifts EBITDA margin to ~48% (FY22-type conversion without FY22 prices), Willow and the LNG projects come on schedule, the FCF breakeven falls to the low-$30s as guided, and capex intensity drops to ~17.5% of revenue.",
    "bear": "OPEC+ unwinds spare capacity into non-OPEC growth (Guyana, Brazil, US shale) and WTI falls to ~$55 from 2027: revenue drops ~12% and margin compresses to ~39-40% as fixed North Slope and oil-sands costs deleverage. Willow runs over budget again and slips, Tier-1 Permian inventory depletes faster than assumed so maintenance capex stays near 19.5% of revenue, and growth fades to ~1%. The balance sheet (A-rated, ~0.6x net debt/EBITDA) survives this comfortably; it is a value case, not a solvency one."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Value the barrels at a mid-cycle price, not at today's print. The owner-FCF build normalises FY2025 (a ~$65 WTI year) and grows it with volume and cost-out, so the valuation is deliberately insensitive to where oil sits on the valuation date. When the share price runs ahead of this number, the market is capitalising an above-mid-cycle oil price.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Oil and gas price: every ~$5/bbl WTI moves revenue ~6-8% and, with high operating leverage, owner FCF considerably more",
      "Willow cost and schedule (already raised to $8.5-9.0bn, first oil early 2029) and Alaska/federal permitting litigation",
      "Qatar NFE/NFS and Port Arthur LNG timing, which drive the FY2028-30 growth leg",
      "Lower 48 Tier-1 inventory depletion raising sustaining capex above ~18% of revenue",
      "Terminal cap at 12x owner FCF binds in the base case; a lower exit multiple for a depleting asset would cut value further"
    ]
  },
